{
  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Perturbations lack reproducible definitions for recent versus older events, coherence matching, deletion size, minimum history length, and receptive-field eligibility.",
    "Causality controls must compare intermediate features after future-token changes; testing only the final position is insufficient. Diagnostic readout checks also cannot verify the separate deployed predictor.",
    "Candidate construction leaves negative count, sampling distribution, history exclusions, repeat targets, and score ties unspecified. Loader batches lack user IDs, preventing reliable user-level pairing and bootstrap intervals.",
    "The adapter is a useful first step, but difficulty 3/10 appears optimistic given architecture inspection, predictor parity, and sampling requirements. Diagnostic sensitivity alone cannot establish improved recommendation quality."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Clarify architecture-specific correctness tests and distinguish checking diagnostic feature extraction from verifying deployed predictions.",
      "query": "NextItNet residual dilated causal convolution implementation: how should tests verify intermediate-position causality, left-padding behavior, receptive-field boundaries across all convolutions and residual paths, and agreement between diagnostic scoring and deployed next-item inference? Which normalization operations can introduce dependence across sequence positions?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Make temporal perturbations reproducible and account for position shifts, last-item identity, and histories that differ from training inputs.",
      "query": "Sequential recommendation history perturbation diagnostics: what controls distinguish recency dependence from last-item identity, sequence-length changes, deletion-induced position shifts, and out-of-distribution histories? How should recent-versus-older deletions be matched by edit size, receptive-field eligibility, and observed interaction coherence?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Specify an interpretable ranking experiment with valid uncertainty estimates while preserving the pinned benchmark.",
      "query": "Sequential recommender training-prefix ranking diagnostics: how should fixed unique negative candidates, previously seen items, repeated positive targets, score ties, and multiple prefixes per user be handled? What user-cluster bootstrap procedure supports paired perturbation comparisons and equal-weight macro averages across four datasets, separately from a fixed sampled validation evaluator?"
    }
  ]
}
